{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.78,"content_digest":"78e696e5a761635f3930ae4d9ab4ab7ef3294534bd2565e5d1817cb65d8f9451","created_at":"2026-08-23T03:59:45+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"3ba072dc5f1e89c7770792ce093e789218888926aa27d334d5eeccfc9e7283b1","input_tokens":4141,"latency_ms":32566,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"b5121ce9a5d10c756d71ea1092196a4859f0f4a29f22c6a059a4d9b78ba44fe4","output_tokens":2625,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:570918bd0d3f98c80afe3892","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:inductive-bias","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:engineering-optimization:01M0PCBGEC1EJ8PM37QVW18YG5","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The meta-principle states that generalization requires inductive bias, with regularization as a canonical form of bias. The roughness penalty in the functional projection is exactly such a bias favoring smooth functions, which enables the representation to cope with noisy response data rather than overfitting the noise.","relation_type":"supports","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T08:08:08+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0PTK9G6V6VXNVFWAWBHMSYT"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T03:59:45+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.85,"content_digest":"80bf0a0b1263086df1ddd69021433b2752835da55491460836483c81761bd31e","created_at":"2026-08-23T11:32:43+00:00","direction":"principle_to_meta","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"c034d741c7667f2ea285cfc98e6ffa1426cd91a7e8cb9742a7393a1c1b8c04d3","input_tokens":5644,"latency_ms":33624,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"e13c159cf02827356d805cc2cb2221602855f3597f2776fa099e7627ca48919f","output_tokens":2320,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:71ced6bdc9006797e0bfffb9","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:bias-variance","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0Q68W2YPBJSW8Z5VN72ARKH","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The cluster-expansion result is a specific instance of the bias-variance decomposition: removing an orbit of clusters is a model restriction whose cost appears as an added squared-bias term m_b V_b^2 in expected squared prediction error. The meta-principle's decomposition directly covers this squared-error bias contribution, while the cluster-expansion assumptions supply the exact form of the omitted-term bias.","relation_type":"specializes","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T15:24:41+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0QKJMJD3A13DCG7YJ3ACW1G"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T11:32:43+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
